  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Arizona received $84.8M in federal contract obligations in FY2027 to date. The state recorded 361 awards with an average award size of $235K. Obligations fell 99.7% year over year compared with prior full fiscal years. Arizona obligations by fiscal year show large prior-year totals: FY22 was $15.3B, FY23 was $19.8B, FY24 was $18B, FY25 was $23.3B, and FY26 was $30.9B. The Department of Veterans Affairs accounted for the largest share of FY2027 to date obligations at $81.5M. FEDEX SUPPLY CHAIN DISTRIBUTION SYSTEM, INC. led vendors with $13.8M, followed by AMERICAN VETERAN CONTRACTING SOLUTIONS LLC at $7.3M and FEDERAL EXPRESS CORPORATION at $7.1M. All Other Business Support Services was the largest industry by obligations at $13.9M, followed by Couriers and Express Delivery Services at $8.4M and Cut Stone and Stone Product Manufacturing at $7.3M. Note that FY2027 totals are partial, fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Why are FY2027 obligations so low compared with prior years?",
        "body": "Arizona obligations for FY2027 to date total $84.8M, compared with FY26 obligations of $30.9B and earlier fiscal years. The state obligations fell 99.7% year over year, which shows FY2027 totals are a partial, fiscal year to date figure rather than a full-year total. Readers should treat FY2027 numbers as incomplete snapshots against full prior-year totals."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies and vendors dominate Arizona federal contracting in FY2027 to date?",
        "body": "The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $81.5M and is the largest buying agency for FY2027 to date. FEDEX SUPPLY CHAIN DISTRIBUTION SYSTEM, INC. received $13.8M, AMERICAN VETERAN CONTRACTING SOLUTIONS LLC received $7.3M, and FEDERAL EXPRESS CORPORATION received $7.1M among the top vendors."
      }
    ]
  },
